// Canonical Swift ops-api package requirements (#937).
//
// This is a Package.swift FRAGMENT, not a manifest: paste the two blocks below
// into your service's own Package.swift. It is the Swift analogue of the Python
// payload's requirements.txt, the Java payload's build.gradle.kts dependency
// block, the Go payload's go.mod.deps and the Node payload's package.json.deps.
//
// Bootstrap adds DEPENDENCIES ONLY. It performs no `targets:` edit — where the
// payload target sits, and which of your targets depends on it, is a structural
// decision about your package that only you can make. See README.md, "Placement".
//
// THE MANIFEST YOU PASTE INTO MUST DECLARE `// swift-tools-version:6.1` OR NEWER,
// on its very first line. The payload is written for the Swift 6 language mode and
// compiles with zero concurrency diagnostics under it; below 6.1 the tools version
// cannot express `.swiftLanguageMode(.v6)` as a per-target setting, so the strict
// checking the payload is verified against silently does not happen.
//
// Platforms floor: .macOS(.v13), .iOS(.v16). Linux has no platform floor to state
// (SwiftPM's `platforms:` covers Apple platforms only) and is the deployment target
// this payload is built for — see README.md, "Linux".
//
// Two packages, ONE instrumentation API. swift-prometheus serves the mandatory
// /metrics pull surface; swift-otel is the OTLP push pipeline. They are not two
// metrics systems: both are swift-metrics BACKENDS, multiplexed behind the single
// `MetricsSystem.bootstrap` the process is allowed. Your code records through
// swift-metrics and lands in both. README.md explains why swift-otel alone cannot
// do this (it ships no Prometheus exporter).
//
// Versions are a FLOOR, not a pin — Renovate/Dependabot move them.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PASTE SITE 1 — into the top-level `dependencies:` array of your Package.swift
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.package(url: "https://github.com/apple/swift-nio.git", from: "2.65.0"),
.package(url: "https://github.com/apple/swift-metrics.git", from: "2.4.1"),
.package(url: "https://github.com/swift-server/swift-prometheus.git", from: "2.3.0"),
.package(url: "https://github.com/swift-server/swift-service-lifecycle.git", from: "2.4.1"),
.package(url: "https://github.com/swift-otel/swift-otel.git", from: "1.5.0"),

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PASTE SITE 2 — into the `dependencies:` array of the TARGET that holds
// Ops/OpsApi.swift (your service target, or a dedicated Ops target)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.product(name: "NIOCore", package: "swift-nio"),
.product(name: "NIOPosix", package: "swift-nio"),
.product(name: "NIOHTTP1", package: "swift-nio"),
.product(name: "Metrics", package: "swift-metrics"),
.product(name: "Prometheus", package: "swift-prometheus"),
.product(name: "ServiceLifecycle", package: "swift-service-lifecycle"),
.product(name: "OTel", package: "swift-otel"),

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// And on that same target, so the payload is checked the way it was verified:
//
//     swiftSettings: [.swiftLanguageMode(.v6)]
//
// swift-otel declares exactly ONE library product, `OTel`, and taking it pulls in
// grpc-swift-2, grpc-swift-nio-transport, grpc-swift-protobuf and swift-protobuf
// whether or not you export over gRPC — the product is not decomposable. That cost
// is stated here rather than discovered in your Package.resolved. README.md,
// "What swift-otel costs", records why it is still the blessed choice.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
